e mouse"], "taxId": "10090", "abbreviation": "MMU", "className": "Species", "schemaClass": "Species"}], "category": "binding", "className": "Reaction", "schemaClass": "Reaction"}], "reviewed": [{"dbId": 9773242, "displayName": "de Martin, R, 2022-05-04", "dateTime": "2022-05-04 04:10:14", "className": "InstanceEdit", "schemaClass": "InstanceEdit"}, {"dbId": 9631054, "displayName": "D'Eustachio, Peter, 2018-12-04", "dateTime": "2018-12-04 19:50:36", "className": "InstanceEdit", "schemaClass": "InstanceEdit"}], "species": [48887], "summation": [{"dbId": 9630927, "displayName": "NF-kappa-B is sequestered in the cytosol of unstimulated cel...", "text": "NF-kappa-B is sequestered in the cytosol of unstimulated cells through the interactions with a class of inhibitor proteins, called NF-kappa-B inhibitors (IkBs, NFKBIA or NFKBIB). IkBs proteins such as NFKBIA, NFKBIB or NFKBIE are characterized by the presence of six to seven ankyrin repeat motifs, which mediate interaction with the Rel homology domain (RHD). RHD mediates DNA binding, dimerization and nuclear localization (Jacobs MD & Harrison SC 1998; Manavalan B et al. 2010). NF-kappa-B inhibitors (IkBs) mask the nuclear localization signal (NLS) of the NF-kappa-B p65 subunit (ReLA, p65) preventing the nuclear translocation of NF-kappa-B (Jacobs MD & Harrison SC 1998; Cervantes CF et al. 2011). A key event in NF-kappa-B activation involves phosphorylation of IkB (at sites equivalent to Ser32 and Ser36 of NFKBIA (IkB-alpha) or Ser19 and Ser22 of NFKBIB (IkB-beta) by the I\u03baB kinase (IKK) complex. The phosphorylated NFKBIA is recognized by the E3 ligase complex leading to K48-linked ubiquitination, and targeted for ubiquitin-mediated proteasomal degradation, releasing the NF-kappa-B dimer p50/p65 (RelA:NFKB1) into the nucleus to turn on target genes (Karin M & Ben-Neriah Y 2000, Kanarek N & Ben-Neriah Y 2012; Hoffmann A et al. 2006). Crystal structures of NF-kappa-B inhibitors:NF-kappaB complexes revealed that an NF-kappa-B dimer binds to one IkB molecule (Jacobs MD & Harrison SC 1998; Ghosh G et 2012).", "literatureReference": [9630944, {"dbId": 2685476, "displayName": "Phosphorylation meets ubiquitination: the control of NF-[kappa]B activity", "title": "Phosphorylation meets ubiquitination: the control of NF-[kappa]B activity", "journal": "Annu. Rev. Immunol.", "pages": "621-63", "pubMedIdentifier": 10837071, "volume": 18, "year": 2000, "url": "http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/10837071", "className": "LiteratureReference", "schemaClass": "LiteratureReference"}, {"dbId": 9630920, "displayName": "Transcriptional regulation via the NF-kappaB signaling module", "title": "Transcriptional regulation via the NF-kappaB signaling module", "journal": "Oncogene", "pages": "6706-16", "pubMedIdentifier": 17072323, "volume": 25, "year": 2006, "url": "http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/17072323", "className": "LiteratureReference", "schemaClass": "LiteratureReference"}, {"dbId": 202538, "displayName": "The two NF-kappaB activation pathways and their role in innate and adaptive immunity", "title": "The two NF-kappaB activation pathways and their role in innate and adaptive immunity", "journal": "Trends Immunol", "pages": "280-8", "pubMedIdentifier": 15145317, "volume": 25, "year": 2004, "url": "http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/15145317", "className": "LiteratureReference", "schemaClass": "LiteratureReference"}, {"dbId": 9630938, "displayName": "The RelA nuclear localization signal folds upon binding to I\u03baB\u03b1", "title": "The RelA nuclear localization signal folds upon binding to I\u03baB\u03b1", "journal": "J. Mol. Biol.", "pages": "754-64", "pubMedIdentifier": 21094161, "volume": 405, "year": 2011, "url": "http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/21094161", "className": "LiteratureReference", "schemaClass": "LiteratureReference"}, {"dbId": 9630922, "displayName": "NF-\u03baB regulation: lessons from structures", "title": "NF-\u03baB regulation: lessons from structures", "journal": "Imm